{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019JcFbbPL1FESob2s9FR1Yr","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Summary\n\nI've successfully retrieved both files. Note that the first file you requested as `prd008-du.yaml` does not exist in the repository. The actual file is `prd009-du.yaml`. Here are the complete contents:\n\n### File 1: `/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/126/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd009-du.yaml`\n\n```yaml\nid: prd009-du\ntitle: \"cmd/du: Recursive Directory Disk Usage\"\n\nproblem: |\n  du reports disk usage for directories and files, but macOS BSD du and GNU du diverge in\n  default block size (512-byte vs 1024-byte blocks), flag availability (-d vs --max-depth,\n  --apparent-size absent in BSD du), and output formatting. Scripts that parse du output\n  break when moving between platforms. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU\n  reference binary (gdu, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all in-scope flag\n  combinations, verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n\n  du requires pkg/sys for extended file metadata (st_blocks for physical block counts,\n  st_dev and st_ino for hard-link deduplication) and pkg/format for human-readable size\n  conversion (-h flag). These shared components are specified in prd002-sys and\n  prd003-format respectively.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Report disk usage for directories by recursively accumulating block counts, matching GNU du default output.\n  - G2: Support summary mode (-s) to print only the total for each argument.\n  - G3: Support human-readable output (-h) matching GNU du -h formatting.\n  - G4: Support all-files mode (-a) to report sizes for individual files, not just directories.\n  - G5: Support depth limiting (-d N / --max-depth=N) to control recursion depth.\n  - G6: Support grand total (-c) to print a cumulative total after all arguments.\n  - G7: Support apparent size (--apparent-size) to report file size instead of block count.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Default behavior\n    items:\n      - R1.1: Must recurse into each directory argument and print the accumulated size and path for each subdirectory and the directory itself. When no arguments are given, must default to the current directory (\".\").\n      - R1.2: |\n          Must report sizes in 1024-byte (1K) block units by default. Each entry's size is fi.Blocks / 2 where fi.Blocks (int64) is the number of 512-byte blocks allocated (st_blocks from syscall.Stat_t). Obtain metadata via pkg/sys.Stat(path string) (*sys.FileInfo, error) — follows symbolic links. sys.FileInfo.Blocks int64 holds the 512-byte block count.\n\n          sys.FileInfo struct (embedded for reference):\n            type FileInfo struct {\n                Mode    os.FileMode\n                Size    int64       // apparent file size in bytes (st_size)\n                Nlink   uint64      // hard-link count (st_nlink)\n                Uid     uint32      // owner user ID (st_uid)\n                Gid     uint32      // owner group ID (st_gid)\n                Rdev    uint64      // device ID for special files (st_rdev)\n                Dev     uint64      // device ID of the containing filesystem (st_dev)\n                Ino     uint64      // inode number (st_ino)\n                Blocks  int64       // 512-byte blocks allocated (st_blocks)\n                Blksize int64       // preferred I/O block size\n                ModTime time.Time   // modification time\n                Sys     os.FileInfo // underlying os.FileInfo\n            }\n      - R1.3: Must print one line per entry in the format \"SIZE\\tPATH\\n\" where SIZE is a right-aligned integer (no leading spaces) and PATH is the relative path as given by the directory traversal.\n      - R1.4: Must not follow symbolic links during traversal. Use pkg/sys.Lstat(path string) (*sys.FileInfo, error) — does not follow symbolic links; returns the symlink's own metadata. pkg/sys.Lstat has the same return type as pkg/sys.Stat (see R1.2 for sys.FileInfo definition).\n      - R1.5: Must process multiple directory arguments in the order given on the command line. Each argument is traversed independently.\n\n  R2:\n    title: Flag behavior\n    items:\n      - R2.1: |\n          -h must display sizes as human-readable strings using binary mode (1024-based) via pkg/format.HumanSize(bytes int64, opts HumanSizeOpts) string with opts.Binary = true (1024-base units, suffixes K/M/G/T/P/E). -h applies to all output lines including the grand total line when -c is also given.\n      - R2.2: -s must print only one line per argument with the total size of that argument. When -s is given, subdirectory lines must not be printed. -s is equivalent to --max-depth=0.\n      - R2.3: -a must print a size line for every file encountered during traversal, not just directories. Without -a, only directory entries are printed (the default).\n      - R2.4: -d N (and the long form --max-depth=N) must limit the depth of reported entries. Depth 0 means only the argument itself (equivalent to -s). Depth 1 means the argument and its immediate children. Files and directories deeper than N are accumulated into their parent's total but not printed as separate lines.\n      - R2.5: -k must report sizes in 1024-byte blocks. Since 1K is already the default, -k has no visible effect but must be accepted without error for compatibility.\n      - R2.6: -m must report sizes in 1048576-byte (1M) blocks. Each entry's st_blocks value is converted from 512-byte blocks to 1M blocks, rounding up.\n      - R2.7: -c must print a grand total line after all arguments are processed. The grand total line has the format \"SIZE\\ttotal\\n\" where SIZE is the sum of all argument totals.\n      - R2.8: --apparent-size must report file sizes using st_size (the apparent file size in bytes) instead of st_blocks (the physical block allocation). The apparent size is then converted to the selected block unit (1K by default, or as overridden by -k, -m, or -h). Obtains st_size from the os.FileInfo.Size() method.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Hard-link deduplication\n    items:\n      - R3.1: Must track files by their device number (st_dev) and inode number (st_ino) across the entire du invocation. A file that has already been counted (same st_dev and st_ino) must not be counted again. This prevents double-counting hard-linked files, matching GNU du behavior (du.c:554 inode tracking via hash table).\n      - R3.2: Must obtain dev and ino via pkg/sys.Stat or pkg/sys.Lstat (see R1.2 for sys.FileInfo). Use sys.FileInfo.Dev uint64 (device ID of the containing filesystem, st_dev) and sys.FileInfo.Ino uint64 (inode number, st_ino) as the deduplication key. The map is keyed by a struct{ Dev, Ino uint64 } pair.\n      - R3.3: Hard-link deduplication applies per du invocation, not per argument. A hard-linked file counted under one argument must not be counted again under a subsequent argument.\n\n  R4:\n    title: Exit codes\n    items:\n      - R4.1: Must exit 0 when all arguments are traversed and reported successfully.\n      - R4.2: Must exit 1 when any error occurs during traversal (cannot stat a file, cannot read a directory, permission denied). Must print a diagnostic to stderr for each error and continue processing remaining entries and arguments before exiting.\n\n  R5:\n    title: Signal handling\n    items:\n      - R5.1: Must install a SIGPIPE handler at startup by calling pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() so that piping du output to head or other truncating consumers does not produce a broken-pipe error message. InstallSIGPIPEHandler uses signal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE) with a buffered channel; a goroutine calls os.Exit(0) on receipt. Must not use signal.Ignore.\n\nnon_goals:\n  - --si (1000-based units) is out of scope; we support only binary (1024-based) human-readable output.\n  - --exclude and --exclude-from pattern-based exclusion filters are out of scope.\n  - -D / --dereference-args (dereference only command-line symlink arguments) is out of scope.\n  - -L / --dereference (dereference all symlinks during traversal) is out of scope.\n  - -l / --count-links (count hard-linked files multiple times) is out of scope.\n  - --time (show modification time alongside size) is out of scope.\n  - --inodes (count inodes instead of block usage) is out of scope.\n  - -b / --bytes (equivalent to --apparent-size --block-size=1) is out of scope as a shorthand; --apparent-size with default 1K blocks is sufficient.\n  - --block-size=SIZE with arbitrary unit suffixes is out of scope; only -k and -m are supported.\n  - -0 / --null (NUL-terminated output) is out of scope.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - \"AC1: Default mode comparison -- gdu and go run ./cmd/du on a test directory fixture produce identical output. Differential tests must use -k to normalize block size and run both binaries on the same directory on the same filesystem.\"\n  - \"AC2: Summary mode -- gdu -s and go run ./cmd/du -s produce identical single-line output per argument on the same fixture.\"\n  - \"AC3: Human-readable sizes -- gdu -h and go run ./cmd/du -h produce identical size strings on a fixture with directories of known cumulative sizes.\"\n  - \"AC4: Hard-link deduplication -- a fixture containing one file hard-linked twice in different subdirectories produces the same total with gdu and the Go binary. The total must count the file's blocks only once.\"\n  - \"AC5: Apparent size -- gdu --apparent-size and go run ./cmd/du --apparent-size produce identical output on a fixture containing a sparse file (where apparent size differs from block allocation).\"\n  - \"AC6: Depth limiting -- gdu -d 1 and go run ./cmd/du -d 1 produce identical output, showing only the top-level entries and the argument total.\"\n  - \"AC7: Grand total -- gdu -c dir1 dir2 and go run ./cmd/du -c dir1 dir2 produce identical output including the \\\"total\\\" line.\"\n  - \"AC8: Exit codes -- the Go binary exits 1 when given a non-existent directory argument and prints a diagnostic to stderr, matching gdu behavior.\"\n  - \"AC9: cmd/du compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n```\n\n### File 2: `/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/126/docs/specs/use-cases/rel01.3-uc001-du.yaml`\n\n```yaml\nid: rel01.3-uc001-du\ntitle: \"Report recursive directory disk usage via du\"\n\nsummary: |\n  The operator runs the Go du binary on a fixture directory to report disk usage. The\n  differential testing harness runs both the Go binary and the Homebrew reference binary\n  (gdu from coreutils) on the same directory on the same filesystem with the same arguments\n  and environment, and confirms that their outputs are identical byte-for-byte. Block counts\n  are deterministic when both binaries stat the same files on the same filesystem. All tests\n  use -k to pin output to 1024-byte blocks and eliminate platform block-size default variance.\n\nactor: Human Operator\n\ntrigger: |\n  The operator has completed the PRD (prd009-du.yaml) and shared component PRDs\n  (pkg/sys, pkg/format from rel00.0) and invokes do-work to produce a passing differential\n  test for cmd/du.\n\nflow:\n  - id: F1\n    step: |\n      The operator runs the Go du binary on a fixture directory with known file sizes and\n      directory structure. Example: ./bin/du -k testdata/du-fixture/\n  - id: F2\n    step: |\n      cmd/du traverses the directory tree using os.ReadDir (or filepath.Walk), calling\n      pkg/sys.Lstat on each entry to obtain st_blocks for block counts and st_dev/st_ino\n      for hard-link deduplication. It accumulates block counts per directory.\n  - id: F3\n    step: |\n      cmd/du writes one line per directory (or per file with -a) in the format\n      \"SIZE\\tPATH\\n\". With -h, sizes are formatted via pkg/format.HumanSize.\n  - id: F4\n    step: |\n      The differential testing harness (pkg/testutils) executes both ./bin/du and gdu with\n      the same arguments, environment, and working directory.\n  - id: F5\n    step: |\n      The harness compares stdout byte-for-byte with no normalization. Both binaries must\n      produce identical size values and path listings because they stat the same files on\n      the same filesystem.\n  - id: F6\n    step: |\n      If the outputs match, the test passes. If they diverge, the harness reports the\n      triggering args, both outputs, and both exit codes.\n\ntouchpoints:\n  - T1: \"cmd/du — recursive traversal, block accumulation, hard-link dedup, flag modes (prd009-du R1, R2, R3, R4, R5)\"\n  - T2: \"pkg/sys — Stat/Lstat for st_blocks, st_dev, st_ino, InstallSIGPIPEHandler\"\n  - T3: \"pkg/format — HumanSize for -h output\"\n  - T4: \"pkg/testutils — RunDiffTests, binary execution and comparison\"\n\nsuccess_criteria:\n  - id: S1\n    criterion: cmd/du compiles and ./bin/du exists after mage build.\n  - id: S2\n    criterion: |\n      Default recursive mode produces one line per subdirectory and the root directory,\n      matching gdu -k output on the same fixture.\n  - id: S3\n    criterion: |\n      -s summary mode produces one line per argument with the total size, matching gdu -sk\n      output.\n  - id: S4\n    criterion: |\n      -a all-files mode produces one line per file and directory, matching gdu -ak output.\n  - id: S5\n    criterion: |\n      -h human-readable mode produces size strings (K/M/G) that match gdu -h output on the\n      same fixture.\n  - id: S6\n    criterion: |\n      -c grand total mode produces a \"total\" line after all arguments, matching gdu -ck output.\n  - id: S7\n    criterion: |\n      Hard-link deduplication works: a fixture with two directory entries pointing to the same\n      inode produces a total that counts the file's blocks only once, matching gdu behavior.\n  - id: S8\n    criterion: |\n      --apparent-size mode uses st_size instead of st_blocks, matching gdu --apparent-size\n      output.\n  - id: S9\n    criterion: |\n      The differential test passes for all flag combinations: default -k, -sk, -ak, -h,\n      -ck, -d 1, --apparent-size, and missing path.\n\nout_of_scope:\n  - Cross-filesystem behavior (files on different mount points) is not tested in the\n    differential harness; both binaries see the same single filesystem.\n  - SIGPIPE handling is specified in prd009-du R5.1 but is not directly testable in the\n    differential harness.\n  - Sparse file creation depends on the filesystem; --apparent-size tests use a regular file\n    with known size rather than a true sparse file.\n\ntest_suite: test-rel01.3\n```\n\nNote: The first file you requested (`prd008-du.yaml`) does not exist in the repository. The actual product requirements document for the du command is `prd009-du.yaml`. If you were looking for `prd008-ls.yaml` instead, please let me know and I can retrieve that file."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a253b187622c073a9 (for resuming to continue this agent's work if needed)\n<usage>total_tokens: 26151\ntool_uses: 12\nduration_ms: 55967</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"06a6dad7-588e-4321-87aa-0af726b7dedc","uuid":"583939ed-32e2-4f41-a497-4ef94b6a7d70","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Read the following files and return their full contents:\n1. docs/specs/product-requirements/prd008-du.yaml\n2. docs/specs/use-cases/rel01.3-uc001-du.yaml\n\nReturn the complete contents of both files.","agentId":"a253b187622c073a9","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Summary\n\nI've successfully retrieved both files. Note that the first file you requested as `prd008-du.yaml` does not exist in the repository. The actual file is `prd009-du.yaml`. Here are the complete contents:\n\n### File 1: `/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/126/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd009-du.yaml`\n\n```yaml\nid: prd009-du\ntitle: \"cmd/du: Recursive Directory Disk Usage\"\n\nproblem: |\n  du reports disk usage for directories and files, but macOS BSD du and GNU du diverge in\n  default block size (512-byte vs 1024-byte blocks), flag availability (-d vs --max-depth,\n  --apparent-size absent in BSD du), and output formatting. Scripts that parse du output\n  break when moving between platforms. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU\n  reference binary (gdu, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all in-scope flag\n  combinations, verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n\n  du requires pkg/sys for extended file metadata (st_blocks for physical block counts,\n  st_dev and st_ino for hard-link deduplication) and pkg/format for human-readable size\n  conversion (-h flag). These shared components are specified in prd002-sys and\n  prd003-format respectively.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Report disk usage for directories by recursively accumulating block counts, matching GNU du default output.\n  - G2: Support summary mode (-s) to print only the total for each argument.\n  - G3: Support human-readable output (-h) matching GNU du -h formatting.\n  - G4: Support all-files mode (-a) to report sizes for individual files, not just directories.\n  - G5: Support depth limiting (-d N / --max-depth=N) to control recursion depth.\n  - G6: Support grand total (-c) to print a cumulative total after all arguments.\n  - G7: Support apparent size (--apparent-size) to report file size instead of block count.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Default behavior\n    items:\n      - R1.1: Must recurse into each directory argument and print the accumulated size and path for each subdirectory and the directory itself. When no arguments are given, must default to the current directory (\".\").\n      - R1.2: |\n          Must report sizes in 1024-byte (1K) block units by default. Each entry's size is fi.Blocks / 2 where fi.Blocks (int64) is the number of 512-byte blocks allocated (st_blocks from syscall.Stat_t). Obtain metadata via pkg/sys.Stat(path string) (*sys.FileInfo, error) — follows symbolic links. sys.FileInfo.Blocks int64 holds the 512-byte block count.\n\n          sys.FileInfo struct (embedded for reference):\n            type FileInfo struct {\n                Mode    os.FileMode\n                Size    int64       // apparent file size in bytes (st_size)\n                Nlink   uint64      // hard-link count (st_nlink)\n                Uid     uint32      // owner user ID (st_uid)\n                Gid     uint32      // owner group ID (st_gid)\n                Rdev    uint64      // device ID for special files (st_rdev)\n                Dev     uint64      // device ID of the containing filesystem (st_dev)\n                Ino     uint64      // inode number (st_ino)\n                Blocks  int64       // 512-byte blocks allocated (st_blocks)\n                Blksize int64       // preferred I/O block size\n                ModTime time.Time   // modification time\n                Sys     os.FileInfo // underlying os.FileInfo\n            }\n      - R1.3: Must print one line per entry in the format \"SIZE\\tPATH\\n\" where SIZE is a right-aligned integer (no leading spaces) and PATH is the relative path as given by the directory traversal.\n      - R1.4: Must not follow symbolic links during traversal. Use pkg/sys.Lstat(path string) (*sys.FileInfo, error) — does not follow symbolic links; returns the symlink's own metadata. pkg/sys.Lstat has the same return type as pkg/sys.Stat (see R1.2 for sys.FileInfo definition).\n      - R1.5: Must process multiple directory arguments in the order given on the command line. Each argument is traversed independently.\n\n  R2:\n    title: Flag behavior\n    items:\n      - R2.1: |\n          -h must display sizes as human-readable strings using binary mode (1024-based) via pkg/format.HumanSize(bytes int64, opts HumanSizeOpts) string with opts.Binary = true (1024-base units, suffixes K/M/G/T/P/E). -h applies to all output lines including the grand total line when -c is also given.\n      - R2.2: -s must print only one line per argument with the total size of that argument. When -s is given, subdirectory lines must not be printed. -s is equivalent to --max-depth=0.\n      - R2.3: -a must print a size line for every file encountered during traversal, not just directories. Without -a, only directory entries are printed (the default).\n      - R2.4: -d N (and the long form --max-depth=N) must limit the depth of reported entries. Depth 0 means only the argument itself (equivalent to -s). Depth 1 means the argument and its immediate children. Files and directories deeper than N are accumulated into their parent's total but not printed as separate lines.\n      - R2.5: -k must report sizes in 1024-byte blocks. Since 1K is already the default, -k has no visible effect but must be accepted without error for compatibility.\n      - R2.6: -m must report sizes in 1048576-byte (1M) blocks. Each entry's st_blocks value is converted from 512-byte blocks to 1M blocks, rounding up.\n      - R2.7: -c must print a grand total line after all arguments are processed. The grand total line has the format \"SIZE\\ttotal\\n\" where SIZE is the sum of all argument totals.\n      - R2.8: --apparent-size must report file sizes using st_size (the apparent file size in bytes) instead of st_blocks (the physical block allocation). The apparent size is then converted to the selected block unit (1K by default, or as overridden by -k, -m, or -h). Obtains st_size from the os.FileInfo.Size() method.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Hard-link deduplication\n    items:\n      - R3.1: Must track files by their device number (st_dev) and inode number (st_ino) across the entire du invocation. A file that has already been counted (same st_dev and st_ino) must not be counted again. This prevents double-counting hard-linked files, matching GNU du behavior (du.c:554 inode tracking via hash table).\n      - R3.2: Must obtain dev and ino via pkg/sys.Stat or pkg/sys.Lstat (see R1.2 for sys.FileInfo). Use sys.FileInfo.Dev uint64 (device ID of the containing filesystem, st_dev) and sys.FileInfo.Ino uint64 (inode number, st_ino) as the deduplication key. The map is keyed by a struct{ Dev, Ino uint64 } pair.\n      - R3.3: Hard-link deduplication applies per du invocation, not per argument. A hard-linked file counted under one argument must not be counted again under a subsequent argument.\n\n  R4:\n    title: Exit codes\n    items:\n      - R4.1: Must exit 0 when all arguments are traversed and reported successfully.\n      - R4.2: Must exit 1 when any error occurs during traversal (cannot stat a file, cannot read a directory, permission denied). Must print a diagnostic to stderr for each error and continue processing remaining entries and arguments before exiting.\n\n  R5:\n    title: Signal handling\n    items:\n      - R5.1: Must install a SIGPIPE handler at startup by calling pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() so that piping du output to head or other truncating consumers does not produce a broken-pipe error message. InstallSIGPIPEHandler uses signal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE) with a buffered channel; a goroutine calls os.Exit(0) on receipt. Must not use signal.Ignore.\n\nnon_goals:\n  - --si (1000-based units) is out of scope; we support only binary (1024-based) human-readable output.\n  - --exclude and --exclude-from pattern-based exclusion filters are out of scope.\n  - -D / --dereference-args (dereference only command-line symlink arguments) is out of scope.\n  - -L / --dereference (dereference all symlinks during traversal) is out of scope.\n  - -l / --count-links (count hard-linked files multiple times) is out of scope.\n  - --time (show modification time alongside size) is out of scope.\n  - --inodes (count inodes instead of block usage) is out of scope.\n  - -b / --bytes (equivalent to --apparent-size --block-size=1) is out of scope as a shorthand; --apparent-size with default 1K blocks is sufficient.\n  - --block-size=SIZE with arbitrary unit suffixes is out of scope; only -k and -m are supported.\n  - -0 / --null (NUL-terminated output) is out of scope.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - \"AC1: Default mode comparison -- gdu and go run ./cmd/du on a test directory fixture produce identical output. Differential tests must use -k to normalize block size and run both binaries on the same directory on the same filesystem.\"\n  - \"AC2: Summary mode -- gdu -s and go run ./cmd/du -s produce identical single-line output per argument on the same fixture.\"\n  - \"AC3: Human-readable sizes -- gdu -h and go run ./cmd/du -h produce identical size strings on a fixture with directories of known cumulative sizes.\"\n  - \"AC4: Hard-link deduplication -- a fixture containing one file hard-linked twice in different subdirectories produces the same total with gdu and the Go binary. The total must count the file's blocks only once.\"\n  - \"AC5: Apparent size -- gdu --apparent-size and go run ./cmd/du --apparent-size produce identical output on a fixture containing a sparse file (where apparent size differs from block allocation).\"\n  - \"AC6: Depth limiting -- gdu -d 1 and go run ./cmd/du -d 1 produce identical output, showing only the top-level entries and the argument total.\"\n  - \"AC7: Grand total -- gdu -c dir1 dir2 and go run ./cmd/du -c dir1 dir2 produce identical output including the \\\"total\\\" line.\"\n  - \"AC8: Exit codes -- the Go binary exits 1 when given a non-existent directory argument and prints a diagnostic to stderr, matching gdu behavior.\"\n  - \"AC9: cmd/du compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n```\n\n### File 2: `/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/126/docs/specs/use-cases/rel01.3-uc001-du.yaml`\n\n```yaml\nid: rel01.3-uc001-du\ntitle: \"Report recursive directory disk usage via du\"\n\nsummary: |\n  The operator runs the Go du binary on a fixture directory to report disk usage. The\n  differential testing harness runs both the Go binary and the Homebrew reference binary\n  (gdu from cor